id: k8s.labels-selectors
kind: kubernetes
title: Labels and selectors
aliases: labels, selectors, matchLabels
tags: operations, discovery
questions:
  - how do objects find each other in Kubernetes?
invariant: Controllers, Services and policy select objects by label equality or set expressions, never by name.
answer_30s: Labels are key/value metadata and selectors are the queries over them; every relationship in Kubernetes (Service to Pods, Deployment to ReplicaSets, NetworkPolicy to Pods) is a selector match.
answer_2m: |
  This is the binding mechanism of the declarative model, so most "nothing is
  connected" bugs are selector bugs. Deployment selectors are immutable after
  creation, which is why a wrong one means recreating the object rather than
  patching it. Use consistent label conventions (app.kubernetes.io/name,
  /instance, /version) because they are also the query interface for
  operations: `-l app=api,version!=v2` is how you scope a rollout, a log read,
  or a delete. Overlapping selectors between two controllers is a real failure
  mode -- two ReplicaSets can fight over the same Pods.
when_to_use:
  - every object; they are the addressing scheme
commands:
  - kubectl get pods -l app=api,version=v2 => only the replicas of one version
  - kubectl get all -l app=api => every object belonging to one application
  - kubectl get pods --show-labels => what labels actually landed on the Pods
failure_modes:
  - Service selector does not match Pod template labels, so endpoints stay empty
  - overlapping selectors between workloads, causing controllers to steal Pods
related:
  - k8s.service
  - k8s.deployment
  - k8s.namespace
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/labels/
